<h2>Why this region anchors our export business</h2>
<p>Short transit times, deep culinary demand for Indian staples, and well-established trade infrastructure make the Gulf and wider Middle East our highest-volume region across nearly every product category we handle — basmati rice above all, but also spices, fresh fruit, pulses and animal feed ingredients.</p>

<h2>What's consistent across the region</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Short transit.</strong> 3–14 days depending on the specific country, versus 18+ days to Europe or 25+ to the Americas. This is what makes perishables like banana and pomegranate viable here in a way they aren't on longer routes.</li>
  <li><strong>Dollar-pegged currencies</strong> across most GCC states, which keeps currency risk low and simplifies pricing.</li>
  <li><strong>Halal considerations</strong> that vary by product and buyer — confirmed per order rather than assumed.</li>
  <li><strong>Established trade infrastructure</strong> — most buyers are experienced importers with clear documentation expectations.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What differs by country</h2>
<p>Saudi Arabia's Ramadan-driven basmati procurement cycle, the UAE's role as a re-export hub, and each country's specific regulatory body (MOCCAE, SFDA, and others) mean the details genuinely differ even within a region that looks uniform from a distance. See the individual country guides below for what's specific to each.</p>
